<Docs title="Installation">
  <h1>Installation</h1>
  <p>
    MixRestore is distributed as a macOS application. Download the latest release and
    you'll be up and running in under a minute.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>macOS 12 (Monterey) or later</li>
    <li>Ableton Live 10, 11, or 12</li>
    <li>Git (included with macOS Command Line Tools)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    Download the latest <code>.dmg</code> from the
    <a href="/download">MixRestore download page</a>.
    You'll need a <a href="https://mixrestore.lemonsqueezy.com/checkout/buy/882ed3c3-f9b8-4841-af50-e7138e18af98?desc=0">license key</a> to activate the app after installing.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the downloaded <code>.dmg</code> file</li>
    <li>Drag <strong>MixRestore</strong> into your Applications folder</li>
    <li>Launch MixRestore from Applications or Spotlight</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>First launch</h3>
  <p>
    On first launch, macOS may show a security prompt since MixRestore isn't notarized yet.
    To proceed:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <strong>System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security</strong></li>
    <li>Scroll down and click <strong>"Open Anyway"</strong> next to the MixRestore message</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Verify Git is available</h2>
  <p>
    MixRestore uses Git for version tracking. Most macOS installations include Git,
    but you can verify by opening Terminal and running:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git --version</code></pre>
  <p>
    If Git isn't installed, macOS will prompt you to install the Command Line Tools.
  </p>
</Docs>
